From 9adaca4faa0a3151262982df69c4977d142ccef6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?K=C3=A9vin=20R=C3=A9mondi=C3=A8re?= <kevin@localhost>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 14:48:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 24/54] vulkan: elide the GDN recurrent-cache CPY
 ([TAG_GDN_ZC])

The GDN kernel already materialises the state snapshots it computes; copying them
into the recurrent ring is pure overhead (452 us/token of CPY in the 125B decode
profile). CUDA does the same in commit 5a460dea: write the cache from the kernel
and drop the copy.

Detection is dependency based rather than adjacency based: a CPY whose source is
a contiguous view of a GATED_DELTA_NET output at the snapshot tail, writing into
a [state, seq, slot] view of the recurrent cache. This matters because the Vulkan
backend reorders nodes for parallelism, which separates the CPY from the GDN by
several nodes (verified: 42 GDN / 49 CPY with graph_optimize, 41/44 without).

The GGML graph is never modified: the CPY node is simply not executed and the GDN
shader receives the cache slice as an extra binding (binding 7, used only when
use_fused_cache is set, aliased to the GDN output otherwise). The cache write is
declared in the backend's sync bookkeeping so ordering against the cache reads
(GET_ROWS) and later writes is unchanged.

Consumers of the CPY result stay correct: any consumer depends on the CPY, hence
on the GDN, so the write always precedes the read.

Shader: binding 7 holds the cache destination; for K>1 the slot stride is the
destination's nb[2], for K=1 only nb[1] is used - both match the strides the CPY
would have used, so the cache layout is bit-identical.

Tests: 6 GATED_DELTA_NET_ZC cases reproduce the production graph (including the
non-contiguous ring view and the K=1 / K>1 paths) and compare CPU against Vulkan
in whole-graph mode. Verified 6/6 with the fusion active (1 CPY elided per case),
6/6 with GGML_VK_DISABLE_FUSION=1 and 36/36 legacy GATED_DELTA_NET unchanged.
On the 27B: 48 cache CPYs elided per graph, byte-identical output with and
without the fusion on both the !keep (K=1) and keep (K>1) paths.
